COMMAND NAME: mpprecoverseg.sh

Recovers a primary or mirror segment instance that has been marked as 
invalid (if mirroring is enabled).


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mpprecoverseg.sh [-d <master_data_directory>] 
                 [-p <spare_hostname> | -s <spare_datadir>] 
                 [-B <parallel_processes>] [-f] 
                 [-z <seg_data_dir>:<seg_hostname>] [-a] [-q] 
                 [-l <logfile_directory>] [-D] [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mpprecoverseg.sh script recovers a failed segment instance if you have 
mirroring enabled. A segment instance can fail for several reasons, such 
as the host goes down, the segment instance process is killed, or the 
data directory gets corrupted. When a segment instance goes down it is 
marked as invalid in the Greenplum Database system catalog. In order to 
bring the segment instance back into operation again, you must first 
correct the problem that made it fail in the first place, and then recover 
the segment instance in the Greenplum Database system.

By default, a failed segment is recovered in place, meaning that the 
system brings the segment back online on the same host and data directory 
location on which it was originally configured. In some cases, this may 
not be possible (for example, if a host was physically damaged and cannot 
be recovered). In this situation, mpprecoverseg.sh allows you to recover 
failed segments to a completely new host (using -p) or on an alternative 
data directory location on your remaining live segment hosts (using -s). 
A spare segment host must be pre-installed with the Greenplum 
Database software installed and configured exactly the same as the existing 
segment hosts. A spare data directory location must exist on all currently 
configured segment hosts and have enough disk space to accomodate the 
failed segments.

The Greenplum Database can run in two modes with regards to mirroring and 
fault tolerance  continue (read-write) mode and read-only mode. This mode 
is set with the mpp_fault_action parameter in postgresql.conf.

If the system is in continue (read-write) mode, this script will shutdown 
the system, copy the data directory of the active segment to the failed 
segment, update the segment dbid and port on the failed segments local 
catalog after the copy, mark the failed segment as valid again in the global 
system catalog, and restart the system.

If the system is in read-only mode, the script will recover the segment 
without shutting down the system. Since the data is read-only, the active 
segment and failed segment would not get out-of-sync in the event of a failure.

If you do not have mirroring enabled, you must take manual steps to recover 
a failed segment instance. To manually mark a segment instance as valid 
again, run the following function on the master:

SELECT mpp_set_segdb_state(<dbid>, <true|false>);

<dbid> is the unique ID for a segment instance. True marks the segment as 
valid and false marks it as invalid.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-p <spare_hostname>

Specifes a spare hostname outside of the currently configured Greenplum 
Database array on which to recover invalid segments. This spare host must 
have the Greenplum Database software installed and configured, and have the 
same hardware and OS configuration as the current segment hosts (same OS 
version, locales, gpadmin user account, data directory locations created, 
ssh keys exchanged, etc.).


-s <spare_datadir>

Specifies a spare data directory location on the currently configured 
segment hosts where you can recover failed segment instances. This file 
system location must exist on all segment hosts in the array and have 
sufficient disk space to accomodate recovered segments.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to recover in parallel. If not specified, the script 
will start up to 4 parallel processes depending on how many segment instances 
it needs to recover.


-f (force shutdown)

Optional. Force shutdown and startup of segment instances marked as invalid.


-z <seg_data_dir>:<seg_hostname> (force recovery of a valid primary)

Forces a recovery of a primary segment instance from its mirror even though 
the segment has not been marked invalid. Use this option with care!


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is still 
written to the log file.

-
l logfile_directory

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Recover any failed segment instances in place:

mpprecoverseg.sh


Recover any failed segment instances to a newly configured spare segment host:

mpprecoverseg.sh -p newhostname


